WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:04.225
This is REPAIR: Beyond Policy
Patches, Distilling Reinforcement

00:04.225 --> 00:07.212
Learning Specialists into
Vision Language Action Policies.

00:08.000 --> 00:10.525
Our key idea is to transfer local robotic

00:10.525 --> 00:13.250
expertise into a single policy for deployment.

00:15.340 --> 00:19.140
A vision language action policy can
handle much of a manipulation task,

00:19.252 --> 00:23.040
yet fail during a brief phase that
demands precise contact or alignment.

00:23.815 --> 00:27.515
A lightweight reinforcement learning
specialist can learn that local skill.

00:28.303 --> 00:32.615
However, keeping specialists at deployment
adds extra controllers and handoffs.

00:33.390 --> 00:36.478
Even successful specialist
execution can be jittery,

00:36.602 --> 00:38.902
as the position traces in the paper show.

00:41.020 --> 00:43.157
This naturally motivates us to distill the

00:43.157 --> 00:45.695
specialists’ expertise back into the VLA.

00:46.482 --> 00:50.295
Simply cloning successful specialist
trajectories leaves two problems.

00:51.082 --> 00:54.132
First, recorded actions can
contain abrupt corrections

00:54.132 --> 00:56.520
and jitter, which become imitation targets.

00:57.295 --> 01:01.045
Second, the student visits states
that differ from the teacher's states.

01:01.820 --> 01:04.907
The paper's feature projection
illustrates this mismatch.

01:05.682 --> 01:08.258
Small student errors can
accumulate in regions where

01:08.258 --> 01:10.795
offline training supplies
too little supervision.

01:12.900 --> 01:15.625
REPAIR uses frozen, phase specific specialists

01:15.625 --> 01:17.925
as teachers for the VLA's action expert.

01:18.700 --> 01:21.000
It begins with an offline warm start using

01:21.000 --> 01:23.637
observations from specialist
assisted rollouts.

01:24.400 --> 01:27.738
It then adds observations from
the student's own execution.

01:28.525 --> 01:31.675
Both stages share the same
value guided supervision rule.

01:32.462 --> 01:36.112
In critical phases, the student
learns from expert candidates.

01:36.887 --> 01:41.400
Outside those phases, replayed VLA
actions help retain existing behavior.

01:43.500 --> 01:45.950
At each critical observation, the selected

01:45.950 --> 01:48.588
specialist proposes multiple action chunks.

01:49.363 --> 01:51.300
Its critic scores the candidates.

01:52.062 --> 01:54.625
REPAIR normalizes these
scores within the candidate

01:54.625 --> 01:56.812
group and converts them into soft weights.

01:57.600 --> 01:59.450
Weighted flow matching then gives higher

01:59.450 --> 02:01.900
value alternatives more influence on training.

02:02.688 --> 02:05.275
This transfers a distribution
of useful actions,

02:05.400 --> 02:08.450
while reducing reliance on any
single recorded correction.

02:10.580 --> 02:13.242
We evaluate REPAIR on six real-robot tasks:

02:13.430 --> 02:16.430
two short-horizon tasks and
four long-horizon tasks.

02:18.540 --> 02:21.765
Each method is evaluated
for 30 trials per task.

02:22.540 --> 02:25.502
REPAIR matches RLT with 30 out of 30 successes

02:25.502 --> 02:28.415
on all six tasks, while
deploying a single VLA.

02:30.540 --> 02:33.678
REPAIR reduces mean
end-effector jerk by 29.8 to

02:33.678 --> 02:37.878
67.5 percent relative to
RLT across all six tasks.

02:38.665 --> 02:41.590
Jerk is measured over
complete successful episodes.

02:43.700 --> 02:46.400
On dual peg insertion,
value-weighted candidates

02:46.400 --> 02:49.438
improve offline success from 60 to 90 percent.

02:50.225 --> 02:53.300
With 15 distillation rollouts,
adding student-visited

02:53.300 --> 02:56.762
states reaches 100 percent
on both evaluated tasks.

02:58.900 --> 03:01.750
Accelerated demonstrations:
stick pick-and-place,

03:01.850 --> 03:04.175
single-arm insertion, and dual-arm insertion.

03:06.300 --> 03:08.987
At ten times speed: grocery bagging, bimanual

03:08.987 --> 03:11.400
table clearing, and toy stowing in a drawer.

03:14.900 --> 03:18.388
REPAIR consolidates local reinforcement
learning expertise through

03:18.388 --> 03:21.862
value guided targets and supervision
on student visited states.

03:22.625 --> 03:25.725
In the reported six task
evaluation, it achieves thirty

03:25.725 --> 03:28.912
successes per task and lower
jerk than specialist takeover.

03:29.700 --> 03:32.350
The final system deploys a single VLA.

03:33.138 --> 03:36.388
More demonstrations are
available on the project website.
